00:17:52.136 --> 00:17:54.110
that I want to be able
to have the permission

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:55.060
to continue to play--

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:56.341
- You're a very unique soul

00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:58.091
and I think, that's the truth,

00:17:58.091 --> 00:17:59.343
because you,

00:18:00.324 --> 00:18:02.390
and we can get into whether you
deal with depression or not,

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:04.550
I don't know, but I think
a lot of people I've met,

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:05.440
they get into this place,

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:06.530
even with "Gangnam Style" right?

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:07.465
I remember when I did that,

00:18:07.465 --> 00:18:08.950
here I am, I'm taking this guy,

00:18:08.950 --> 00:18:09.783
who is my brother now,

00:18:09.783 --> 00:18:11.320
me and him, me and Psy have this thing,

00:18:11.320 --> 00:18:13.073
we joke around, we actually
say this to each other,

00:18:13.073 --> 00:18:13.910
instead of just saying I love you,

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:14.970
we say love you, fuck you,

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:16.420
that's a joke,

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:17.763
it's a joke between us.

00:18:18.711 --> 00:18:19.800
- Where is he living right now?

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:20.680
- He lives in Korea

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:21.676
and he goes back and forth to Beijing

00:18:21.676 --> 00:18:24.730
and he does a bunch of stuff
between the two but Psy's great

00:18:24.730 --> 00:18:26.850
but the difference is is like

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:28.276
Psy was like, "I have to do it again,

00:18:28.276 --> 00:18:29.942
"I have to do it again." People were like,

00:18:29.942 --> 00:18:32.240
"oh that was good but
can you do it again?"

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:33.970
Actually, Gentlemen went number three,

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:35.146
which was the next one

00:18:35.146 --> 00:18:36.603
but the funny thing is

00:18:36.603 --> 00:18:39.492
I never sought out to do it again.

00:18:39.492 --> 00:18:42.999
I had a slightly overweight Korean man

00:18:42.999 --> 00:18:47.432
dancing and singing in
Korean, fully in Korean,

00:18:47.432 --> 00:18:49.270
and he's in his 40s

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:52.068
and he's coming to the United States.

00:18:52.068 --> 00:18:54.570
I wanted to get that to be
number one in the United States

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:55.403
and around the world.

00:18:55.403 --> 00:18:56.236
We did that.

00:18:56.236 --> 00:18:58.090
It stayed there for multiple weeks.

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:00.960
That was the impossible

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:01.793
and that's what people do,

00:19:01.793 --> 00:19:03.337
you achieve the impossible and they go,

00:19:03.337 --> 00:19:04.930
"Oh that was cool but can you do this?"

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:07.000
And I think if you start
to get in that mold

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.980
of the expectations of
others and what they want,

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:12.313
you miss the point that you
already achieved greatness

00:19:12.313 --> 00:19:14.080
and you have to live in that too.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:15.340
So I understand what you're saying,

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:16.670
I think the problem with me and Gary

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:17.968
is we always say the same thing

00:19:17.968 --> 00:19:20.298
but we say it in
completely different ways.

00:19:20.298 --> 00:19:24.590
- Look, I only think I admire you so much

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:25.670
because of our similarities

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:27.120
'cause I love myself so much.

00:19:30.278 --> 00:19:33.110
- You play a large part in how
I got back in shape though.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:34.510
I actually wanted to give you love to that

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:36.870
because I'm gonna live years longer

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:39.380
for my boys and my daughter because of you

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:43.738
because I watched Gary hire a trainer 24/7

00:19:43.738 --> 00:19:45.590
and I was like, you know what,

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:47.616
I'm in a place financially
where I can do that.

00:19:47.616 --> 00:19:48.560
- [Gary] And it makes so much sense.

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:49.890
- And I need to kickstart myself.

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:51.100
So I hired a trainer

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:52.780
to stay with me for 30 straight days

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:53.613
day in and day out.

00:19:53.613 --> 00:19:55.472
- Dude, there was some photo
you posted on Instagram

00:19:55.472 --> 00:19:57.655
where I was like, fuck,

00:19:57.655 --> 00:19:59.270
you got into shape quickly

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:00.780
and when you do the right things

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:03.920
but man you had some real
fucking muscles in one photo.

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:05.314
I was like, fuck that's a lot of muscles.

00:20:05.314 --> 00:20:07.976
- You know what, I got Irv's DNA.

00:20:07.976 --> 00:20:10.990
My dad is 64, has a six pack,

00:20:10.990 --> 00:20:12.270
he's like 200 pounds, six foot two,

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:13.120
he's a big dude

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.230
and I got his DNA

00:20:14.230 --> 00:20:15.063
so I was able to,

00:20:15.063 --> 00:20:16.380
my friend Eugene,

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:17.330
who I had Rumble with,

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:18.163
he was giving me shit,

00:20:18.163 --> 00:20:19.689
he was like, "I worked out like you,

00:20:19.689 --> 00:20:20.522
"why is this not happening?"

00:20:20.522 --> 00:20:22.380
And I was like, I have friends who

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:24.046
it doesn't happen for me the
way it happened for them.

00:20:24.046 --> 00:20:25.010
- I was excited to see you guys do,

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:26.940
you guys went into a pretty
significant partnership, right?

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:28.780
'Cause he's really,

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:29.930
I like him a lot.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:30.763
- He's a great guy

00:20:30.763 --> 00:20:31.783
and I'm really excited for--

00:20:31.783 --> 00:20:33.143
- Did you know him in the catch days

00:20:33.143 --> 00:20:34.674
and even before that, the promoter days?

00:20:34.674 --> 00:20:35.507
- [Scooter] Yeah.

00:20:35.507 --> 00:20:36.560
- So you've known him a long time?

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:37.930
- 10 June.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:39.500
- Scooter, what is it,

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:40.930
let me go completely left field

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:42.680
on the back of that Eugene comment,

00:20:43.786 --> 00:20:45.100
you and I are different in one way,

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:48.750
you have been in the,

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:49.876
you've grown up in the scene,

00:20:49.876 --> 00:20:52.260
and grew in the scene

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:54.560
meaning I did something very weird,

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:56.730
I literally worked in a
liquor store in New Jersey,

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:59.470
never went to Manhattan,
LA, Atlanta, anywhere,

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:02.316
literally for 14, until I
was like 34, 35 years old.

00:21:02.316 --> 00:21:05.280
I've been fascinated with,

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:08.250
I keep bringing up these
throwback kind of things

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.299
because much like Eugene as well,

00:21:10.299 --> 00:21:12.900
I was like oh,

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:14.920
not that I envy, I was like
oh, it would've been cool

00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:16.300
to come up with all these people

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:17.317
and like see everybody,

00:21:17.317 --> 00:21:21.300
I came out of left field kind
of in a lot of ways into it.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:23.270
What does it feel like to,

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:25.560
how do you look at things
like Eugene and others,

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:28.700
when you see a lot of people
that you came up with,

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:30.750
some accelerated, some fell off,

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.464
some did better than you
thought in your own fun game,

00:21:33.464 --> 00:21:34.480
what does it feel like

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:38.180
to be part of a scene and
culture for two decades

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:39.416
but still be so young

00:21:39.416 --> 00:21:42.510
where you know there's another
three, four, five chapters?

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:43.700
I'm sure you look at some of your friends

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:45.970
who are 38 and say, "Wow,
she's gonna be the governor

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:47.317
"of California one day,

00:21:47.317 --> 00:21:49.110
"that one's gonna be this,"

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:52.100
how does it feel to be
part of the zeitgeist,

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:53.327
that's the way I would put it?

00:21:53.327 --> 00:21:54.710
- Look, it's cool

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:57.130
'cause there's history with certain people

00:21:57.130 --> 00:21:58.070
and you're rooting for each other

00:21:58.070 --> 00:21:59.340
and you kind of come up

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:02.860
and there's a respect 'cause you know--

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:03.820
- They grinded.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:04.929
- You know that they grinded--

00:22:04.929 --> 00:22:06.130
- That's what I like

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:07.270
- And you know the mentality,

00:22:07.270 --> 00:22:08.880
like Eugene, like me,

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:11.110
has never been someone to
party at his own parties.

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:13.231
He was a worker from day one

00:22:13.231 --> 00:22:15.300
and a Russian immigrant

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:16.824
who made it happen

00:22:16.824 --> 00:22:18.771
and came to the city
and saw the opportunity

00:22:18.771 --> 00:22:20.210
and grinded it out.

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:21.720
- Wait, he was like in Ohio
or something like that?

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:23.070
- No, he was just coming into the city.

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:24.300
- But do you know where he was,

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:25.240
okay, go ahead.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:26.460
- I think he grew up around here.

00:22:26.460 --> 00:22:27.910
- Got it.

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:31.440
- And I think for me,

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:32.417
that's really great

00:22:32.417 --> 00:22:34.053
but at the same time,

00:22:34.969 --> 00:22:39.969
I also get upset when I see
certain people from that scene

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.300
who, they find resentment in that

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:44.043
and I just--

00:22:44.043 --> 00:22:46.220
- [Gary] Because they
didn't make it as big

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:47.850
and they were ahead of other people?

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:49.281
- Sometimes it's they were ahead,

00:22:49.281 --> 00:22:50.114
they didn't make it as big.

00:22:50.114 --> 00:22:51.546
Other times they are making it big

00:22:51.546 --> 00:22:54.190
but they've made the mistake

00:22:54.190 --> 00:22:55.873
of thinking that, and I see
it with people I've known,

00:22:55.873 --> 00:22:57.690
see it with people that I don't know,

00:22:57.690 --> 00:22:58.523
they make the mistake

00:22:58.523 --> 00:23:00.550
of thinking success is
some kind of zero sum game.

00:23:00.550 --> 00:23:01.383
- Yeah, I'm blown away

00:23:01.383 --> 00:23:02.216
by people's inability

00:23:02.216 --> 00:23:03.763
to understand the abundance.

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:06.531
I'm shocked by that.

00:23:06.531 --> 00:23:08.830
- Well I've never
understood it because I--

00:23:08.830 --> 00:23:09.920
- It's not practical.

00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:10.753
- It's not practical

00:23:10.753 --> 00:23:12.900
and it think the way I've
figured out how to explain it

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:14.509
makes it easier for people to understand,

00:23:14.509 --> 00:23:16.080
because we grew up in a world

00:23:16.080 --> 00:23:19.190
where we think that if I'm winning,

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:20.117
someone else should be losing

00:23:20.117 --> 00:23:22.160
or if I'm losing, someone else is winning

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:23.610
and that's why people get into that mode

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.400
and what I like to explain is,

00:23:25.400 --> 00:23:26.913
I call it the vacation theory

00:23:26.913 --> 00:23:29.217
where if I'm successful

00:23:29.217 --> 00:23:31.330
and none of my friends are successful,

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:33.780
I have to pay for them to
go on vacation with me.

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.520
I don't want to pay for your vacation.

00:23:36.520 --> 00:23:39.050
My goal in life is to
have all of my friends

00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:40.260
become successful

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:43.090
so that they also can
pay for the nice vacation

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:45.293
and it isn't all on me financially.

00:23:45.293 --> 00:23:47.953
So if you think of it that way,

00:23:47.953 --> 00:23:50.170
because I think a lot of people,
they kind of look into it,

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.020
like I want to be the one who makes it.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:54.653
No you want everyone to make
it and the other thing I'll say

00:23:54.653 --> 00:23:56.690
is whenever I talk to powerful people,

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:58.540
and I had the pleasure today of sitting

00:23:58.540 --> 00:23:59.481
with a mentor of mine today,

00:23:59.481 --> 00:24:01.500
who's had an incredible career,

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:02.700
he's one of my favorite
people in the world,

00:24:02.700 --> 00:24:05.980
and when he talks about
other super powerful people,

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:09.220
he talks about 30, 40, 50 years of history

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:11.367
and what I think the
beautiful part about that is

00:24:11.367 --> 00:24:13.480
people make this this idea

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:15.105
that I gotta get to this person.

00:24:15.105 --> 00:24:17.043
They think they gotta get to you.

00:24:17.043 --> 00:24:18.690
They think they gotta get to me.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:19.640
You want to know who you gotta get to?

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:21.590
They're sitting next to
you in the living room

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:22.775
dreaming just like you.

00:24:22.775 --> 00:24:24.342
They are your peers.

00:24:24.342 --> 00:24:25.943
If you come up,

00:24:25.943 --> 00:24:27.230
anyone who's powerful,

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:29.033
their network is more
powerful than their money.

00:24:29.033 --> 00:24:30.727
They can say, "Oh, who do you need?

00:24:30.727 --> 00:24:32.878
"I've known him for 20 years,
let me make a phone call."

00:24:32.878 --> 00:24:34.740
It's coming up, that grind.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.760
Me and Eugene, we went
into this business together

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:40.140
because we knew each other from the grind.

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:44.250
I can call, when I needed Ludacris on Baby

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:45.810
back in he day for Justin's first single

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:46.860
that would give him some credibility,

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:48.440
it was because Ludacris and I came up

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:50.336
with Throw Them Bows in a night club.

00:24:50.336 --> 00:24:55.150
So for me, it's not about the
person you aspire to know.

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:57.056
You should go to them for stories,

00:24:57.056 --> 00:24:58.460
get some wisdom.

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:00.320
But if you want to know who
you need to network with,

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:02.203
network with your peers
and help each other rise up

00:25:02.203 --> 00:25:03.520
and that's where power comes from.

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:04.490
- Scooter, let me ask you a question.

00:25:04.490 --> 00:25:07.125
What do you think you're
dramatically better at,

00:25:07.125 --> 00:25:09.451
as a dude, as human,

00:25:09.451 --> 00:25:11.470
what do you think you're
way better at today

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:13.020
than even just five years ago?

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:14.433
- Watching the Jets lose.

00:25:15.388 --> 00:25:17.198
I'm much better at it now--
- We're very good at that.

00:25:17.198 --> 00:25:19.071
On a serious note--
- Than I was five years ago.

00:25:19.071 --> 00:25:21.750
- Because what I think is
super interesting about my,

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:22.583
I don't even know why,

00:25:22.583 --> 00:25:24.010
I'm intuitively asking this

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:26.100
because again listen,

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:27.440
we're so busy,

00:25:27.440 --> 00:25:29.640
we don't get to spend
that much time together.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:30.958
- I know the answer.

00:25:30.958 --> 00:25:31.926
- You know the answer

00:25:31.926 --> 00:25:32.759
to the question?
- I know the answer

00:25:32.759 --> 00:25:34.543
'cause I had to go to a
very dark place to find it.

00:25:34.543 --> 00:25:36.343
- Tell me 'cause I'm really curious.

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:41.300
- So you know a lot
about me and my family.

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:44.710
So two years ago I lost one
of the most important people

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:46.900
in my life which was my grandmother,

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:49.480
and she was one of the
most special human beings

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:50.740
anyone's ever met,

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:52.972
and everyone who knew her loved her

00:25:52.972 --> 00:25:56.120
and she was an Auschwitz
Holocaust survivor

00:25:56.120 --> 00:25:57.760
so she saw the worst of humanity

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.040
but literally was the light

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:00.410
in every room she went into

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.078
and everyone called her Ma.

00:26:02.078 --> 00:26:03.720
When she passed away,

00:26:03.720 --> 00:26:06.020
my brother, 11 hours later had twins,

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:09.050
and nine days later I had my second son.

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:14.050
So now I got a wife with a 21
month old baby and a newborn,

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:16.230
she's breast feeding,

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:17.840
I got a dad who's broken

00:26:17.840 --> 00:26:19.265
because both his parents are gone

00:26:19.265 --> 00:26:21.856
and I'm trying to be the
rock for the whole family

00:26:21.856 --> 00:26:24.320
and I didn't have time for grieving.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:26.850
So I started going into a depression

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:28.057
which I wasn't used to.

00:26:28.057 --> 00:26:30.087
I had gone to different
dark places in the past

00:26:30.087 --> 00:26:31.480
but I couldn't shake this one,

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:32.560
I couldn't sleep it off,

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:34.607
I couldn't get away from it.

00:26:34.607 --> 00:26:36.760
And I started to get even more depressed

00:26:36.760 --> 00:26:39.900
because no one was
asking me if I was okay.

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:41.110
Because I'd been the rock

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:44.350
for all of my friends and
my family my entire life,

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:45.830
I'm always okay,

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:47.461
I'm always the mature one,

00:26:47.461 --> 00:26:48.930
I have fun, I go crazy

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:49.880
but I was always the one

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:51.210
that you didn't have to worry about.

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:52.130
And I'm always the one

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:54.510
who goes and fixes
everyone else's problem.

00:26:54.510 --> 00:26:56.617
And finally a friend of mine
I opened up to and he said,

00:26:56.617 --> 00:26:58.070
"You need to talk to
your wife about it too."

00:26:58.070 --> 00:26:59.050
And I went and talked to my wife

00:26:59.050 --> 00:26:59.937
and I expected her to be like,

00:26:59.937 --> 00:27:01.048
